<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  HeroesEvolve settings.

  Delete this file and the mod runs on the defaults below. Delete a single
  line and that one value falls back. A value the mod cannot read, or one
  outside its sane range, is clamped or ignored rather than crashing a
  campaign.

  Nothing here is stored in your save, so changing it takes effect on the
  next load and uninstalling the mod leaves no trace.
-->

<HeroesEvolve>

  <!-- ============ What runs ============ -->

  <!-- Gives a starting kit to a lord who has none: too few weapons to fight
       with, or still in the clothes he came of age in. It covers a lord a
       captor has stripped as well, once he is free again, so the same rule
       dresses a man however he came to have nothing. -->
  <EnableRepair>true</EnableRepair>

  <!-- Grows every lord's skills over the years, so a hero does not end up
       weaker than the troops he leads. -->
  <EnableSkillGrowth>true</EnableSkillGrowth>

  <!-- New campaigns only, and only with EnableSkillGrowth on. Before the first
       day, every lord's skills are set where his age and talent put him, so
       young lords start young and grow into their strength. Saves already under
       way are never touched. -->
  <StartLordsOnCurve>true</StartLordsOnCurve>

  <!-- Lets lords buy better gear when they visit a town. Never changes what
       kind of fighter a lord is: it replaces what he already carries with
       something better of the same kind, and never touches an empty slot. -->
  <EnablePurchases>true</EnablePurchases>

  <!-- Writes hev.log under
       %LOCALAPPDATA%\Mount and Blade II Bannerlord\logs\ -->
  <EnableLogging>true</EnableLogging>


  <!-- ============ How good a lord's gear may get ============ -->

  <!-- Combat skill points that buy one tier of gear. LOWER MEANS BETTER
       EQUIPPED LORDS, and this is the setting to reach for first.

       28 puts the average lord one tier above what the game already gave
       him, which leaves the top end to be decided by his clan's money
       rather than by this number. 40 was the old value and makes lords
       noticeably poorer than vanilla dresses them. -->
  <SkillPerTier>28</SkillPerTier>

  <!-- Weight of clan standing against personal skill in that ceiling.

       Zero, from measurement rather than taste: across a young and a mature
       campaign, clan tier neither predicted what a lord wears nor separated
       the population, since by midgame every clan is tier 4 or better. Set
       it above zero if you want great houses to outfit their lords better
       regardless of merit. -->
  <ClanWeight>0.0</ClanWeight>
  <SkillWeight>1.0</SkillWeight>

  <!-- Nobody is capped below this, however unskilled. -->
  <MinimumTier>1</MinimumTier>

  <!-- How far a ranged skill must lead a lord's melee skills before his
       starting kit commits him to a bow or a crossbow. -->
  <DominanceMargin>30</DominanceMargin>


  <!-- Whether starting kits and shopping reach the heroes of your own clan: your
       family, and the companions leading your parties and caravans. True
       because a party your brother leads is one the AI takes into battle, and
       the argument for equipping any other lord applies to him.

       Set it false if you outfit them by hand and want that respected. Heroes
       travelling inside your own party are never touched either way, since
       their inventory is already yours to manage. Their skills keep growing
       regardless: that takes nothing from you. -->
  <ManageOwnClan>true</ManageOwnClan>

  <!-- ============ Money ============ -->

  <!-- The share of his wallet a lord may spend on ONE TRIP to the market,
       divided between the slots that town can improve for him. This is what
       makes wealth matter, and what decides how a lord gets better: a man who
       needs everything spreads it thin and comes back in middling gear, while a
       man who needs one thing spends it all on that one thing. He improves by
       buying fewer pieces of better quality.

       At 0.30 a house of median wealth re-equips a robbed lord at tier 4, and
       reaches tier 6 once he is down to his last slot or two.

       CHANGED MEANING. This used to cap a single piece, which said nothing
       about how much a man needed: a lord who had lost his whole kit got the
       same allowance for his first piece as one replacing a strap, and could
       buy a magnificent helmet and then eleven rags. If you carry a value over
       from an older install, raise it — a tenth per trip is far stingier than a
       tenth per piece was. -->
  <SpendingShare>0.30</SpendingShare>

  <!-- The commission a caravan's leader keeps out of what it earns, and the only
       money he buys his own gear with.

       A caravan master is the one hero who is not a clan leader and still has
       an income anyone can measure, so he buys his gear out of a cut of it
       rather than out of his patron's treasury. A caravan that trades well arms
       the man running it; a poor one leaves him plain; and he can never outspend
       what he has produced.

       At 0.20 a caravan earning a thousand a day pays its master about 16,800 a
       year. Against what 1,560 purchases actually cost, that buys a full tier-3
       kit in a little over a year, tier 4 in about three and tier 5 in about six
       — if his skills allow those tiers at all, and if his caravan is not
       robbed first, which early in a campaign happens about once a year.

       Nothing is taken while he has nothing left to improve: once every piece he
       wears is as good as his skills and the markets allow, the commission stops,
       and it starts again the day his skills raise his ceiling. A weak leader
       capped at tier 2 used to bank half his caravan's income for nothing.

       At 1.00 he keeps everything the caravan makes and it stops paying its
       owner at all. At 0.00 he is paid nothing, spends down what he already has,
       and then buys no more. It does NOT hand him back the clan purse: his
       patron's treasury is not his to spend at any setting.

       Bear in mind that he is stripped of what he wears when his caravan is
       beaten, so arming him well is also what makes losing him expensive.

       Leaders of war parties are deliberately untouched by this. A war party
       does not exist to make money, so rationing its leader by what he earns
       would measure him against a target he was never given. -->
  <CaravanGearShare>0.20</CaravanGearShare>

  <!-- Scales the gold held back so buying gear can never stop a clan paying
       its troops. 1.0 keeps exactly the game's own threshold per war party.
       Zero removes the safety net. -->
  <ReserveMultiplier>1.0</ReserveMultiplier>

  <!-- Chance a lord bothers with the market when he walks into a town. He
       takes at most one shopping trip a day and buys what that town has for
       him, so this decides how often he walks past a market rather than how
       much he leaves with. At 1.0 he stops at every town he enters. -->
  <ShopChancePerVisit>0.25</ShopChancePerVisit>

  <!-- Whose colours a lord favours when he buys: clan (his clan's culture),
       hero (his own culture) or none (no preference: the best piece wins,
       whoever made it). A piece in the favoured culture counts a quarter of
       itself better, so a lord abroad still buys foreign gear when it is
       clearly the better piece, while a well-dressed lord at home needs a very
       large improvement to leave his own colours. Repair always dresses a lord
       as his own people. -->
  <ShoppingCulture>hero</ShoppingCulture>

  <!-- A wall instead of a preference: with this on, a lord never buys armour
       (or horse harness) that is not the favoured culture's. OFF BY DEFAULT,
       because the catalogue is uneven — aserai, khuzait and nord have no leg
       armour on sale at all, so their lords would keep whatever boots they
       arrived in for the rest of the campaign. Unassigned pieces, which belong
       to no culture, are always allowed. -->
  <OwnCultureArmorOnly>false</OwnCultureArmorOnly>

  <!-- The same wall for weapons. A lord abroad with nothing of his own on the
       shelf simply buys nothing that trip. -->
  <OwnCultureWeaponsOnly>false</OwnCultureWeaponsOnly>


  <!-- ============ Losing gear ============ -->

  <!-- Lets a captor go through his prisoner's kit and keep it. ON BY
       DEFAULT. It was off while the system was unproven, on the grounds that
       it changes the feel of every defeat, but a campaign has since run it
       through seventy-one robberies without an error, and a feature nobody
       switches on is a feature nobody has. Set it to false for the old shape.

       The gear is moved, never destroyed: it goes into the captor's own
       inventory, so beating him gets it back. That includes the unique gear
       that makes a noble look like one — Caladog's gilded armour is a trophy
       like anything else — and an AI captor sells it on at the next town, so
       it circulates rather than rotting in a baggage train. Only quest items
       are safe. A lord never trades his OWN unique gear away when shopping.

       Who robs is character, not dice. Bandits always do. A merciful and
       generous lord never does. Dishonour, closefistedness and roguery raise
       the odds; goodwill between the two men lowers them; a father will not
       strip his son unless the two of them are already at war. A lord who
       robs loses standing with the man he robbed.

       You are the exception on both sides: nothing decides to rob for you,
       and your own standing never moves for having been robbed. Relations in
       this game are yours to earn. -->
  <EnableCaptureLoss>true</EnableCaptureLoss>

  <!-- Scales how often a captor robs. 0 is the same as off, 0.5 is the
       default, 1 runs the character rules at full strength, 2 doubles them.

       Halved after measurement. At full strength a census found 83 robberies
       among 405 lords in seven months — a third of the nobility stripped every
       year — which made robbery the largest single influence on what a lord
       wears, ahead of his skill, his clan's wealth and the market put together.
       The purchase engine was working; it simply could not build faster than
       this tore down. At 0.5 it shapes the map without ruling it. -->
  <PlunderChance>0.5</PlunderChance>

  <!-- You rob by asking. When EnableCaptureLoss is on and you are holding a
       captured lord, talking to him offers "Hand over your arms and armour."
       Nothing rolls dice on your behalf: taking his gear is a thing you do,
       and it costs you Honor and Mercy through the game's own hostile-action
       penalty, standing with him and with his kin, and it earns you Roguery.

       AI lords have no such conversation, so their roll IS their decision —
       made by their character, which is the whole point of the rules above. -->

  <!-- Item ids this mod will never buy or grant, separated by commas or
       newlines. Empty by default.

       This is here because the mod shops in a catalogue it does not own. It
       hunts for the best thing on a town's shelf, which makes it only as
       balanced as the shelf: any mod can add an outlier, and five hundred
       lords will find it faster than you will.

       One whole class of outlier is already handled without this list. Any
       weapon carrying an incendiary physics material is refused outright —
       see ItemCatalog.IsIncendiary. Every such item in the game is meant to be
       off the market (siege pots, burning ballista bolts, the cheirosiphon and
       its ammunition), and Open Source Weaponry's "Calradic Fire Pots" was the
       single exception in the whole catalogue: 200 blunt damage in stacks of
       five, on sale for 36,842. Lords bought it. They no longer can, and
       nothing needs listing here for that.

       Use this for anything else you would rather your lords never carried.
       The id is the one in the mod's own XML, matched case-insensitively:

         AR_naptha_pot, some_other_item                                    -->
  <ExcludedItems></ExcludedItems>

</HeroesEvolve>
